    "q-2913": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2913",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{{t}^{8}{y}^{6}}$ given that $y>0.$",
      "worked_solution": "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{8}{y}^{6}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{4}{y}^{3}\\cdot {t}^{4}{y}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{({t}^{4}{y}^{3})}^{2}} & = \\\\ |{t}^{4}{y}^{3}| & = \\\\ |{t}^{4}|\\cdot |{y}^{3}| & = \\\\ |{t}^{4}|\\cdot |y{|}^{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn even power of a number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have $|{t}^{4}|={t}^{4}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we are told that $y>0,$ and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have $|y|=y.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{t}^{4}|\\cdot |y{|}^{3}={t}^{4}\\cdot {y}^{3}={t}^{4}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
