    "q-52504": {
      "dom_id": "step-q52504",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}}\\,$ given that $y<0.$",
      "worked_solution": "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{y}\\cdot \\frac{3}{y}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{y})}^{2}} & = \\\\ |\\frac{3}{y}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|3|}{|y|} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y<0.$ To take the absolute value of any negative number we need to flip the sign, so we have $|y|=-y.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{|y|}=-\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
